---
title: Fudge brownies
servings: 24
yield: 24 two-inch brownies
tags: [american, vegetarian]
cuisine: american
time: 40 minutes
source: Fudge Brownies by PJ Hamel, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/fudge-brownies-recipe
---

Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Lightly grease a #9x13-inch pan{}. To lift the
slab out whole, line it with parchment and grease that too.

In a #stand mixer{}, beat the @eggs{4}, @Dutch-process cocoa{1.25%cup},
@table salt{1%tsp}, @baking powder{1%tsp}, @espresso powder{1%tsp}(optional) and
@vanilla extract{1%tbsp} on medium ~{1%minute}, until thick and mostly smooth.

Melt the @unsalted butter{16%tbsp} in a #saucepan{} over low heat. Stir in the
@granulated sugar{2.25%cup} and keep heating until hot, 110 to 120°F, and shiny,
but not bubbling.

Stir the hot butter and sugar into the egg mixture until smooth. Add the
@all-purpose flour{1.5%cup} and @chocolate chips{2%cup} and stir until smooth,
scraping the bowl.

Spread evenly in the pan. Bake ~{28%minutes} to 32 minutes, until the edges are
set and the centre is moist but not raw: a knife tip poked into the middle
should show moist crumbs, no wet batter.

Cool on a rack before cutting.

> Heating the sugar with the butter, and the chips, give the shiny top crust.
> Natural cocoa makes a denser, sharper brownie.
